      en: "Budapest, the capital of Hungary, was formed in 1873 through the unification of the cities of Buda, Pest, and Óbuda, and has since grown into a major European hub. The city is bisected by the Danube River, with the hilly Buda district on the right bank and the flat Pest side on the left, connected by iconic structures like the Chain Bridge. Budapest's history dates back to the Roman settlement of Aquincum in the 1st century AD and flourished as a royal seat and a co-capital of the Austro-Hungarian Empire. Today, it is a significant political, cultural, and economic center in Central Europe, home to approximately 1.7 million residents. Often called the \"City of Spas,\" Budapest is world-famous for its historic thermal baths, including the Széchenyi and Gellért complexes. The city's architecture is a stunning display of Neo-Gothic, Renaissance, and Art Nouveau styles, with several areas designated as UNESCO World Heritage sites. From its vibrant ruin bars to its world-class opera and fine dining, Budapest offers a captivating blend of imperial grandeur and contemporary urban energy.",
      es: "Budapest, la espléndida capital de Hungría, está dividida por el majestuoso Danubio en la montañosa Buda y la vibrante Pest. La ciudad surgió en 1873 por la unión de Buda, Pest y Óbuda y cuenta con una historia milenaria que abarca desde los romanos hasta la época de la monarquía austrohúngara. Los visitantes quedan fascinados por la diversidad arquitectónica, que va desde la gótica Iglesia de Matías hasta el neogótico edificio del Parlamento y magníficos edificios modernistas. Budapest es también la única capital del mundo que cuenta con una gran cantidad de manantiales termales, lo que la convierte en un centro de la cultura termal europea. Las orillas del Danubio, el barrio del castillo y la avenida Andrássy son Patrimonio de la Humanidad de la UNESCO y dan fe del pasado glorioso de la metrópoli.",
